# ERC-4337 EntryPoint v0.7 on Arc Testnet — identity check by prediction
#
# Address:  0x0000000071727De22E5E9d8BAf0edAc6f37da032
# Chain:    Arc Testnet, 5042002
# Date:     2026-08-26
# Command:  see userophash.py, which prints both the prediction and the cast call
#
# Arc's paymaster page says to verify the EntryPoint v0.7 address before use.
# `cast code` had already shown 16,035 bytes deployed there, which proves a contract
# is present and nothing about which one, and comparing a codehash needs a published
# reference this project's tooling cannot fetch.
#
# So the identity was established the other way round: v0.7's getUserOpHash is fully
# specified, so its answer for a chosen input is derivable off-chain. `userophash.py`
# derives it from first principles with a standard-library keccak256 checked against
# three published vectors, then hand-builds the calldata.
#
# PREDICTED, before the call:
#   selector                          0x22cdde4c   (v0.7 PackedUserOperation; v0.6's
#                                                   UserOperation is 0xa6193531 —
#                                                   a different function entirely)
#   UserOperationLib.hash(zeroed op)  0xdf7116b815dffbc9c574fdaaedb0c5745acd15415f5c500ea1a9d1b425c85449
#   getUserOpHash(zeroed op)          0x07f96d30b0cc2f1c5f6381309cbc63d3c80eb361a777ff3b7d29ea473e06c930
#
# Raw output follows. balanceOf answering 0 rather than reverting shows the deposit
# ABI is present; the payer has never deposited, so 0 is the correct answer.

== balanceOf(payer) ==
0
== getUserOpHash(zeroed PackedUserOperation) ==
0x07f96d30b0cc2f1c5f6381309cbc63d3c80eb361a777ff3b7d29ea473e06c930

# MATCH, all 32 bytes. The deployed code implements v0.7's hashing algorithm over
# v0.7's ABI, keyed to this EntryPoint address and this chain ID — the returned value
# commits to both, so the same bytecode at a different address, or on a different
# chain, could not produce this number.
#
# What this still does not prove: that the whole 16,035 bytes are byte-identical to
# the canonical v0.7 release. It proves the hashing path is v0.7's. That is the part
# a paymaster integration depends on for signature compatibility, and it is a
# materially stronger claim than "code exists at the documented address".
#
# Same method as expected-id.txt: write the number down first, then ask the chain.
